# Candidate operators for the OperatorSwap sub-mutator.
#
# One operator per line, TAGGED with its arity family:
#
#     <family>: <token>
#
# Recognised families:
#   infix   binary infix operators (arity 2) — arithmetic, bitwise, shift,
#           comparison. All bind tighter than `not`, so any infix token may
#           replace any other without a syntax error (`a < b` -> `a @ b` -> `a in
#           b`); at worst the expression regroups, which is useful new structure.
#   bool    `and` / `or`. Isolated from `infix`: these bind *below* `not` and
#           comparison, so their operands can be things (`not d`, `a < b`) that a
#           tighter operator rejects — `a and not d` -> `a < not d` is a
#           SyntaxError. So `and`/`or` only ever swap with each other.
#   prefix  unary prefix operators (arity 1) — `+ - ~ not`. Interchangeable with
#           each other but NOT with a binary token (arity differs).
#
# Untagged lines (and lines whose family keyword isn't recognised) are ignored:
# an operator of unknown arity can't be swapped in without risking broken syntax.
# Editing this file broadens mutation coverage without recompiling the mutator
# logic (it is include_str!'d into the binary).

# --- infix: arithmetic / bitwise ---
infix: +
infix: -
infix: *
infix: @
infix: /
infix: //
infix: %
infix: **
infix: <<
infix: >>
infix: &
infix: |
infix: ^

# --- infix: comparison ---
infix: <
infix: >
infix: <=
infix: >=
infix: ==
infix: !=
infix: is
infix: is not
infix: in
infix: not in

# --- bool: boolean (isolated pool; see header) ---
bool: and
bool: or

# --- prefix: unary ---
prefix: +
prefix: -
prefix: ~
prefix: not

# --- assign: statement assignment operators ---
# `a = 1` <-> `a += 1` / `a **= 1` / ... Only single simple targets (Name /
# Attribute / Subscript) are ever swapped, so no tuple/multi-target breakage.
assign: =
assign: +=
assign: -=
assign: *=
assign: @=
assign: /=
assign: //=
assign: %=
assign: **=
assign: <<=
assign: >>=
assign: &=
assign: |=
assign: ^=
